# libcanberra -- plays desktop event sounds, and is what mutter's
# -Dsound_player=true resolves.
#
# mutter meson.build:190-193 gates it on have_sound_player, which defaults true,
# and then takes dependency('libcanberra', version: '>= 0.26'). This package is
# the player; the SOUNDS come from sound-theme-freedesktop, packaged alongside,
# and neither is any use without the other -- see the note there.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://0pointer.de/lennart/projects/libcanberra/libcanberra-0.30.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=c2b671e67e0c288a69fc33dc1b6f1b534d07882c2aceed37004bf48c601afa72
SRC_DIR=libcanberra-0.30

# THE PATCH IS BACK, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS A CONSEQUENCE RATHER THAN A DECISION.
#
# patches/ carries BLFS 12.4's libcanberra-0.30-wayland-1.patch, upstream commit
# c0620e432650, "gtk: Don't assume all GdkDisplays are GdkX11Displays". Its own
# header says it fixes "a bug crashing some applications in Wayland desktops",
# and this tree runs no X session at all, so the assumption it removes is false
# for every session Duct boots.
#
# It was dropped when the GTK helper was disabled, because it touches
# src/canberra-gtk-module.c and src/canberra-gtk.c and NOTHING ELSE -- exactly
# the two files that were no longer compiled. That recipe recorded "IF THE GTK
# HELPER IS EVER ENABLED HERE, THAT PATCH MUST COME BACK." It has, so it is.
#
# Upstream sha256 e48ce8f952c2b71e38866b95aa21e02437924ed6f9c4409b64e8e4cb392b9013,
# recorded as PROVENANCE rather than as a fetch guard: the patch is committed
# under patches/ and applied by _scripts/prepare.sh, so nothing downloads it and
# its integrity is git's.

# --disable-oss: OSS has not been the Linux audio interface for twenty years and
# BLFS disables it too.
#
# --disable-gstreamer and --disable-pulse. BOTH DEFAULT TO **auto** in
# configure.ac (the gstreamer block at 375, pulse at 301), and neither is
# packaged here, so both would silently resolve to "off" on their own. Pinned
# because a soft-skip that happens to do what is wanted is not a decision --
# and because if a gstreamer stack is ever packaged for something else, this
# would quietly grow a dependency on it without anyone choosing that.
#
# alsa is left ENABLED, its default, and is the backend that actually plays
# sound here: alsa-lib is packaged.
#
# --enable-gtk3 IS BACK, AND THE HISTORY IS THE POINT.
#
# This recipe first left --enable-gtk3 at its `auto` default, having examined it
# and recorded that the helper "costs one small library". CI then failed on both
# architectures:
#
#     src/canberra-gtk-module.c:28:10: fatal error: gdk/gdkx.h: No such file or directory
#
# gdk/gdkx.h is GTK 3's X11 BACKEND header, and gtk3 was then built
# WAYLAND-ONLY. So the helper was disabled, with the reasoning that nothing in
# this tree uses ca_gtk_* anyway.
#
# THAT SECOND REASONING WAS ALSO SCOPED. gnome-settings-daemon 48.1
# meson.build:109 is
#
#     libcanberra_gtk_dep = dependency('libcanberra-gtk3')
#
# unconditional, no required:false. The helper is not unused -- its consumer had
# simply not been packaged yet when the check was made. Same shape as
# gnome-desktop's legacy library, discovered in the same scan.
#
# The blocker was real and is now gone: gtk3 3.24.50-2 is built WITH its X11
# backend and published, so gdk/gdkx.h exists. That flip was made for mutter's
# X11 frames helper and serves this too -- one toolkit correction, two
# consumers.
#
# --disable-gtk stays: that is the GTK 2 helper, and GTK 2 is not packaged.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --disable-oss --disable-gstreamer --disable-pulse \
--enable-gtk3 --disable-gtk"
